0|1@relationship_advice^1440831720#cujj8g4:thepowerwithinnn$:You didn't do your part to take on your responsibilities as the man of the house, so it was only a matter of time before this was going to happen. You shouldn't have let her just control things, because nobody lives life without making mistakes. Simply putting forth your perspective as a male would have been a great relief to her, and you guys certainly could have avoided some pitfalls with your input. It's important that you believe that you know best how to run your life, so that you can make your own decisions and gain experience. In doing so, you develop the capacity to assess whether others are making good decisions or not, and to start demonstrating to your girlfriend that you're not going to let everything go to shit because you, in fact, might actually know better this time around. From there, she can come to respect you. She was hoping that you would step up and put forth real standards in the relationship, in the house, and in your own life, at the very least. Instead, you let her tread all over you because you didn't establish healthy boundaries. You also repeatedly gave into her and let anxiety over potential infidelity emasculate you. She's been who's determining whether this relationship is off or on, and that's just very stressful for a woman because she begins to feel like you aren't an equal partner in this crazy life. She doesn't know that this is why she's unsatisfied with you, but it's got a lot to do with it. You can give her all the love and affection in the world, but without also managing the level of attraction she feels toward you, things will never work out. She's exasperated and wants a break to go be submissive and let a man take the reins, if only for a short while. You need to work on yourself. Stop letting external factors and other people determine your self-esteem. You aren't a victim, so stop acting like one.
1|0@relationship_advice^1440871426#cujwxud:Thereg123$:No I shouldn't of let her have so much control, but it probably would of ended it much sooner, which could of been the best thing. Like I said before she rarely communicated with me in the past, even when I communicated my issues with her. I did establish some ground rules in the beginning of the relationship and she treaded on some of them so.. yeah I should of taken the reins there instead of being "Well i'm sure it will get better". Seems I was being more of a "Nice" guy instead of a "Good" guy. My parents always instilled "Treat a woman with respect, love her and cherish her." but I should of taught myself along the road to stand my ground. I understand now what truly are some major faults I have thanks to you guys and I am grateful. Thanks.
8|0@relationship_advice^1440832668#cujjhjf:starsinaparsec$:It sounds like she already gave you a second chance back in March, but things didn't change. It's frustrating being in a relationship where you feel like your partner is holding you back, or when you feel like you need to parent them because they don't have their shit together. When you see your behavior changing in a negative way because of your partner sometimes is a good thing to get out. This is a good opportunity for you though. You learned that you need to step it up and try harder at life. If you had that much debt and you can pay it off in a couple months then why were you just sitting around paying interest? If you knew she wanted the bills on her desk then why didn't you walk the extra little distance? Put a little more effort into being a responsible person. Whether she comes back or not it'll make you a better person. 
1|0@relationship_advice^1440868708#cujvhp4:Thereg123$:Yes it can be frustrating if that is what she feels. But like I said in my post she very rarely communicated with me about her feelings and issues. I've told her countless times over the years that she needs to be more open with me about things regardless if she thinks there trivial. I've done it with her but she always would say "I'm fine." or "Nope, i'm happy.". So those things she complained about were never mentioned to me until the day we broke up (EX: the bill). I learned though at least what I need to do in my life to become a better man and to find someone who also believes in following certain ground rules and is willing to actually communiicate. Thanks for the advice.
1|0@relationship_advice^1440869068#cujvokg:thepowerwithinnn$:She doesn't quite know why she's unhappy, but she does know the magnitude of her unhappiness. And because of this, she's going to tell a white lie and say she's fine. In the short term, it's much better to do that than to tell the truth but not have any good reasons for you.
2|0@relationship_advice^1440851536#cujnt6b:TrustMe_IAmAwesome$:I think all of those reasons she gave you are things that annoyed her, but the only reason she said them was because you kept asking"why?" They are not why she broke up with you. She told you why this time and she told why back in March. She doesn't love you any more and doesn't want to be with you anymore. Take her for her word on this. The first step to healing will be to accept this fact. Even if all the other reasons are true, at the end of the day, she doesn't want to be with you. Sitting there ruminating over all the things you did wrong and thinking how you can change things won't change the very simple truth, that she doesn't want to be with you anymore. You say you love her. Do you want her to be in a relationship that she doesn't want to be in, just to spare your feelings? Of course you don't. So accept that it's over; the relationship has run its course. While you work on acceptance, try to get yourself out of the negative feedback look you're in. Go to the gym and get some endorphins running through you. Even if you don't feel like it, trust me, just a walk around the neighborhood and some sunlight will help your mood. And try to find something you enjoy to occupy yourself. Read something, get a new video game, colour. I'm sure you had hobbies before you met her, rekindle your passion for something that makes you happy. Best of luck in everything. Breaking up is hard, but you are capable of working through it. 
1|0@relationship_advice^1440869977#cujw5tl:Thereg123$:Agreed, theres plenty of things she did that annoyed me as well but I knew that I could get past those flaws because I loved her. Never kept asking why just asked once and she gave me an answer (i'm just on reddit posting about it because theres nothing much else to do right now and I like hearing peoples opinions on things). Yeah breaking up sucks as usual. Agreed on her being happy by not being with me. That's why I never asked her to give it another shot. Have to let go the ones you love for them to be happy, it hurts alot but I accepted it. Theres alot of details I left out about things she has done that has really hurt me over the years but people already read my sob story and I rather focus on how I could improve. Maybe it's best this way, for both of us. Thank you for the advice. Oh and i'm a workout freak so I got that covered ;p
